WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.940
Glass Onion is a treasure trove of riches,
with secret cameos, clues even Benoit Blanc

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.260
missed, and easter eggs and call-backs to
the first Knives Out, including a hidden wheel

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.260
of knives!

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.550
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and I've
been re-watching and pausing the Knives Out

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.869
sequel to uncover all the killer details and
things you might have missed.

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:23.080
Spoilers ahead of course, so take care.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.450
When we first meet Blanc in the sequel, pause
and you can spot a book called "Cain's Jawbone",

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.189
which is one of the world's most complex literary
murder mysteries.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:37.370
The book contains just one hundred pages,
all in the wrong order, which have to be rearranged

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.090
to solve six murders.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.010
Since its publication in 1930 only three people
are known to have solved it.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.180
With its extreme complexity, it certainly
seems like the type of puzzle Blanc would

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.770
relish solving, very unlike the mystery he
eventually solves in Glass Onion.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.070
It's so dumb, it's brilliant.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.070
No!

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.070
It's just dumb!

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.739
The book's disordered pages might also be
a nod to how the movie reveals its story out

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:07.480
of order, with flashbacks to different periods
as the plot and its various twists are revealed.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:12.490
The design on Miles's puzzle box invitation
card is reminiscent of the evil eye commonly

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.950
found in Greece and around the Mediterranean.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:19.610
The eye can be considered either a curse or
a protective symbol against those who'd wish

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.960
you harm, and I think it's interesting that
Miles also has these huge circular installations

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.680
in all of the guest bedrooms, with the blue
one in Dukes' room specifically reminding

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.579
me of the image on the invites.

00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:36.310
Given that Miles kills Duke and threatens
his other friends to get them to support his

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:41.670
launch of Klear or take sole responsibility
for a sweatshop, the eyes on the invitation

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.600
definitely feel like more of a curse from
the billionaire than a protective symbol.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.329
When the so-called Disruptors arrive on the
island, Miles is playing the Beatles song

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:56.159
"Blackbird" and he even claims the guitar
is the original owned by Paul McCartney.

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:58.950
Serenading me with my song!

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.350
On the guitar Paul wrote it on.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:05.260
However, there's a clue that Miles may be
lying about this as, if you go back and pause

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:11.440
the scene, notice that he's playing a right-handed
guitar, whereas Paul McCartney is left-handed.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.460
Although McCartney did sometimes play a right-handed
guitar, I suspect this is a hint that Miles

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.239
is really a phoney and the guitar is a fake,
as we'll also later discover is the case when

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:24.180
it comes to the origin of his business empire.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.050
Also note how he just dumps the instrument
on the sand, which is a little odd if it really

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.620
is an original Beatles guitar.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.660
Either way, it all goes to show that Miles
is more concerned with ego than authenticity.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:42.239
You probably noticed some of the crazy artwork
in and around Miles's mansion from the billionaire's

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:47.319
shirtless portrait inspired by Fight Club,
a film which starred Edward Norton alongside

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:51.019
Brad Pitt;
to the mural of Kanye West dressed as a Roman

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:53.260
Senator;
all of which are a commentary on the type

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.560
of person Miles likes to imagine himself to
be.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.200
But hidden in the bathroom where Blanc and
Helen conspire are a couple of paintings with

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.799
some secret symbolism.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:08.120
The one behind Blanc is "Icarus" by Matisse,
referring to the Greek legend of the boy who

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.159
flew too close to sun.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:15.340
Like Icarus, who was warned the sun would
melt the beeswax in his wings, Miles too was

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:21.150
warned about his unstable hydrogen technology
which ends up blowing up his mansion and melting

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.690
the Mona Lisa.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.540
Appropriately, the body in the painting also
resembles a chalk outline from a murder scene

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.680
and the red heart could be a nod to both the
red square in the Fibonacci spiral that gives

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:37.620
away the location of the red envelope, and
also the spot on Helen's chest where she's

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:38.930
shot later on.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.819
Meanwhile, the painting behind Helen is "The
Absinthe Drinker" by Degas, which feels humorously

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:49.050
fitting as right at this moment she discovers
she's been getting drunk on Jared Leto's hard

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:50.050
kombucha.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.050
This isn't a drink.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:52.489
This is some funky health stuff.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:54.200
No, you give me that.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.220
That's 9% alcohol.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.730
Leto's kombucha is a fictional creation for
Glass Onion, but later on you can see it's

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.500
called "Red Planet", referencing the name
of the actor's real-life band "Thirty Seconds

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.670
To Mars".

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:10.379
The glass statues which Helen and the others
end up gleefully smashing also have some hidden

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:12.710
easter eggs according to Rian Johnson.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:17.299
This circular one resembles the memorable
wheel of knives from the first movie and it's

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:20.880
Peg who eventually ends up smashing that piece.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.509
When Miles reveals he hired Gillian Flynn
of Gone Girl fame to write the plot of his

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:30.000
murder mystery weekend, it's a shout-out to
the plot of Agatha Christie's "Dead Man's

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.199
Folly" where a wealthy man asks a mystery
novelist to stage a Murder Hunt for him.

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:40.130
And Miles deciding to kill Duke, after he
tries to blackmail him over keeping quiet

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.729
about the breaking news of Andi's death, is
very similar to how in Christie's novel the

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:49.090
murderers kill someone who's blackmailing
them over a previous murder.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:52.630
And the fact that Miles mispronounces Gillian
Flynn's name...

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.930
I hired Gillian Flynn to write the whole thing.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:59.190
is in keeping with the many malapropisms he
spouts over the weekend.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.730
That's the infraction point.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:05.460
When Blanc and Miles are discussing the famous
detective's surprise arrival on the island,

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:06.539
Blanc says…

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:11.669
Mr. Bron, I've learned through bitter experience
an anonymous invitation is not to be trifled

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:12.669
with.

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:16.770
That's a call back to the first movie, where
Ransom hired Blanc anonymously in order to

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.660
try and pin the murder of his grandfather
Harlan on his nurse Marta.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.150
When Daniel Craig arrives at the pool all
covered up, this feels like a deliberate joke

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.800
about his debut as James Bond in Casino Royale
and that shirtless scene at the beach where

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.300
he emerges from the water, much like previous
Bond girl Ursula Andress.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.920
I think Blanc's clothing is a very deliberate
choice especially given the contrast with

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.400
Whiskey's swimming pool moment shortly before.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.930
There's a clever call-back to the first movie
in the scene where Blanc and Helen discover

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.639
Serena Williams is available for training
in Miles's private gym.

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:55.780
Pause the moment here and notice that the
renowned tennis player is reading "Gravity's

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:00.199
Rainbow", the novel that Blanc mentioned in
the first film when explaining his approach

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:02.010
to detective work to Marta.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.610
I anticipate the terminus of Gravity's Rainbow.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:06.759
I haven't read it.

00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:08.090
Neither have I.
Nobody has.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:09.449
But I like the title.

00:06:09.449 --> 00:06:13.699
Rian Johnson has said that Agatha Christie
has been a huge influence on him and the Knives

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:19.360
Out movies, so when Miles spikes his own drink
with pineapple juice and passes it to Duke,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.540
this is a hat-tip to "The Mirror Crack'd"
where the killer adds poison to her own glass

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:26.180
before handing it to her victim.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:31.099
Angela Lansbury starred as Miss Marple in
that movie and makes a cool cameo in Glass

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:35.280
Onion as one of Blanc's famous friends who
discovers he's the imposter in the game of

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.620
"Among Us" they're playing.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.520
I saw you go in the engine room.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.520
You're the Imposter.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:40.520
We all know it.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.710
This is a fun bit of foreshadowing for Blanc's
role in Glass Onion.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:50.870
Also, notice how in the game Lansbury is suitably
named "MSheSolved", a nod to her iconic role

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:54.400
as Jessica Fletcher in "Murder, She Wrote".

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.590
Composer Stephen Sondheim, who also cameos
in this scene

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:59.090
Sorry, Blanc, you're thrown out of the airlock.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:00.300
It's a no-brainer.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:06.330
co-wrote the whodunit movie "The Last of Sheila",
which Rian Johnson pays homage to in Glass

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:09.310
Onion's set-up where the Disruptors arrive
on the dock.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:13.500
Also in the scene is Natasha Lyonne who will
be starring in Johnson's upcoming detective

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.910
series "Poker Face", and former basketball
player Kareem Abdul-Jabbar is co-author of

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.660
a series of mystery novels starring Mycroft
Holmes.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.360
There's another Agatha Christie-esque moment
in Glass Onion when Helen uses Jeremy Renner's

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.580
hot sauce as fake blood and when Miles also
uses fake blood in his murder mystery, both

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:38.050
of which remind me of Death on the Nile, where
red ink is used to fake having been shot.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:43.620
Now, because like Blanc, many of us expected
a densely-layered mystery in Glass Onion,

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.340
the movie has fun laying out a lot of red
herrings, one of the biggest of which is the

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:49.780
mysterious character Derol...

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:53.229
I'm not here.
...who keeps popping up, saying he's not doing

00:07:53.229 --> 00:07:54.229
anything...

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:55.229
Hey.

00:07:55.229 --> 00:07:56.900
Not here.
...and in the end he really isn't.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:01.879
He's played by actor Noah Segan, who was also
Trooper Wagner in the first Knives Out movie.

00:08:01.879 --> 00:08:06.289
According to Rian Johnson, Derol is based
on Kato Kaelin, an actor who was staying at

00:08:06.289 --> 00:08:07.289
O.J.

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:12.430
Simpson's estate on the night of the 1994
murders, and Derol performs a similar role

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.300
in Glass Onion.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:19.379
Noah Segan regularly appears in Johnson's
movies, and in Derol's room you can spot an

00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:24.099
easter egg on his surfboard to the midnight
tunnel symbol from the director's first feature

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:25.770
film Brick.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:31.169
Another regular Rian Johnson cameo in Glass
Onion is Joseph Gordon-Levitt who's the voice

00:08:31.169 --> 00:08:33.750
of Miles's ridiculous hourly dongs.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.750
Dong!

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:36.320
You know I got Phil Glass to compose that?

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.320
I got Phil Glass to compose that.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.510
Philip Glass is a famous composer known
– suitably enough

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.180
– for his "music with repetitive structures".

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:47.130
And it's entirely in keeping with Miles's
stupidity and vanity that he would hire someone

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:51.440
like Glass to compose something as inane as
a gong sound.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.980
By the way, Gordon-Levitt has appeared in
some form in all of Johnson's films and in

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:00.070
the first Knives Out he was the voice of the
detective in the murder mystery series that

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:01.360
Marta's sister was watching.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.420
You crushed his skull with a forklift and
burned his hands off to erase the fingerprints!

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:11.769
Glass Onion is clearly set during the pandemic,
with some amusing details like Birdie's Lana

00:09:11.769 --> 00:09:17.490
del Rey mask, and Ethan Hawke's brief cameo
credited as "Efficient Man" who delivers a

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:18.850
mysterious shot of something.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:21.370
Is that some kind of disinfectant or…

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:22.370
You're good.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:27.490
A nice detail is that when Hugh Grant opens
Blanc's door he has a jar of sourdough starter

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:31.580
in his hand, a nod to the bread-making craze
during the first lockdown.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:37.750
Now, Miles had personalised glasses for everyone
including himself, but Birdie's long-suffering

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:41.500
assistant Peg only gets a generic red plastic
cup.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:46.120
But an interesting little detail you can spot
if you pause just here is that Peg has written

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:50.310
her name on the cup herself, not wanting to
be left out.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:55.149
When Helen ransacks everyone's rooms looking
for her sister's red envelope, as she tips

00:09:55.149 --> 00:10:00.940
out Duke's toiletries you'll see he has three
boxes of Viagra, a wink to how he infamously

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:05.630
got banned from Twitch for selling pills that
supposedly contained rhino horn;

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:09.450
pills which obviously didn't work as he doesn't
use them himself.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.980
Zero rhino in those pills.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:16.620
When Miles reveals he's got hold of the real
Mona Lisa, he talks about how her enigmatic

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.300
smile changes each time you view her.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:20.820
Her smile's there, then it disappears.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:21.820
Is she happy?

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:22.820
Is she sad?

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:23.820
Is it something else?

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:28.460
This might be a little stretch, but this could
also be referencing the first Knives Out where

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:33.310
Harlan's portrait changes from its serious
demeanour early on, to a smiling one at the

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.200
very end after his killer's been caught.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:41.329
And a really nice detail is how the very final
shot of the new movie is set up with Helen

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:45.810
sitting perfectly poised, which feels very
Mona-Lisa-like.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:50.410
The Beatles song "Glass Onion" plays over
the movie's final credits and there are several

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:53.380
layers of meaning to this particular song
choice.

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:58.440
Similar to how the Glass Onion sequel is purposely
full of red herrings, John Lennon deliberately

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.019
wrote the lyrics to the song Glass Onion to
poke fun at anyone who'd over-analysed the

00:11:03.019 --> 00:11:07.990
meaning of famous Beatles songs, so it's full
of lyrical call-backs such as "The walrus

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:09.029
was Paul".

00:11:09.029 --> 00:11:13.130
Rian Johnson has also revealed that there
are Glass Onion lyrics hidden in the glass

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.200
artwork in Miles's mansion.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:19.880
I spotted these glass flowers referring to
the "bent-back tulips" line and I think this

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:21.740
is a hat-tip to "Lady Madonna".

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:26.610
By the way, Johnson came up with the film's
title when he decided the story would be about

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:31.300
a billionaire living in a transparent building
then looked for a song with the word glass

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.350
in it, and the Beatles track was the first
he discovered.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:39.770
Now, there are also more hidden clues that
give away the multiple twists in Glass Onion.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:44.240
One I missed until some of our amazing movie
lovers pointed it out on my last video is

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.560
that although Helen's made up to look like
her twin Andi, there's a subtle difference

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:52.800
as Helen's hair parts on the right, whereas
Andi's parts on the left.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.560
And if you want to discover all the other
clues and secret symbolism in the movie, tap

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.130
here to watch my "Clues You Missed" video.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:01.450
There's also a link in the video description.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:05.880
So, what other easter eggs or cool details
did you spot in Glass Onion?

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.880
Let me know in the comments below.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:12.060
Tap here to discover all the clues you might
have missed to the movie's big twists.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:15.570
And if you enjoyed this video, do leave a
thumbs-up, I really appreciate it.

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:19.730
Thanks for enjoying Glass Onion with me and
hope you have a marvellous movie-loving week!

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:20.930
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

